{"authored" [{"question" "q-339641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x>0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}?$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote is $y=$\n\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|3-8x|=8x-3.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x>0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8-\\frac{3}{x})}{(4+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a8fc4a62db3ddd630a473cdc2c920f268b90c9706f019f17762a4e08b4154258" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339641" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-339641" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-35831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-35831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14}{(6+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{6+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{7}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-35831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a8fc4a62db3ddd630a473cdc2c920f268b90c9706f019f17762a4e08b4154258" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-35831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha256" "794a5b783a2e6ed149688ecb4d1509c96dad9ea40a695092ba12b68371dbf4c9" "basis" 655 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-35831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964951/state.json" "file_sha25